# `prod-deploy` is opt-in, and the user opts in — not you

Pushing to `main` queues the `migrate` job, which runs `liquibase-deploy.sh`
with `--context-filter=prod-deploy` against production Aurora before any
container restarts. That job sits in the `production-db` environment behind a
required reviewer, so it waits for a human — but `context` still decides
whether a changeset is in that batch at all.

| `context=` | Push to `main` | Manual `database.yml` (no filter) |
|---|---|---|
| `"bt"` (schema only) | skipped — **the default you write** | applied |
| `"bt,prod-deploy"` | queued for `migrate`, applied on approval | applied |
| omitted | **queued too** — no context matches *every* filter | applied |

Write the schema context alone. Then tell the user the changeset is staged and
ask whether to add `prod-deploy`. Only add it once they say yes. The reviewer
gate is a backstop against mistakes, not a substitute for asking: it shows a
job name, not a diff, so an approver cannot see what they are approving.

```xml
<!-- ✅ Default: staged, waits for a human -->
<changeSet id="1.17.0-001-sp-get-thing" author="alfcheun" context="bt" runOnChange="true">

<!-- ⚠️ Only after the user confirms this specific release -->
<changeSet id="1.17.0-001-sp-get-thing" author="alfcheun" context="bt,prod-deploy" runOnChange="true">
```

Never omit `context` to "keep it out" of the deploy — that does the opposite.

## Holding a changeset back is not a dead end

A schema-only changeset stays pending until someone runs the **database**
workflow with `action=deploy` and types `deploy` into `confirm_deploy`. That
run applies every pending changeset regardless of context. Say this when you
report the work, so the user knows the migration is waiting rather than lost.

## Editing a procedure `.sql` is itself a production migration

48 of the 49 files under `*/procedures/` and `*/functions/` are already covered
by an active `runOnChange` changeset tagged `prod-deploy` — that coverage is
deliberate, so an edit redeploys rather than going quiet. The consequence: a
one-line change to a procedure body queues a production migration on the next
push, with no new changeset involved. Treat any edit under
`db/liquidbase/*/procedures/` as a production change and say so before making it.

## Say what a release will actually do

Give the blast radius before the user decides: which schemas, how many
changesets, and whether any replace a procedure body. Sweeping "re-apply every
procedure" releases are the risky shape — one killed the 2026-08-16 deploy 15
changesets into `BT`. Prefer the narrowest set of files that fixes the problem.
